# Sum-denominator lemma (informal proof sketch; own reasoning, not checked by anyone else)

Setting. v_1 < ... < v_n positive integers, gcd = 1 (WLOG after Galilean shift and scaling,
as in Bohman–Holzman–Kleitman / Tao). g_i(t) = ||v_i t|| (distance to nearest integer),
f(t) = min_i g_i(t), ML(v) = max_{t in [0,1)} f(t). f is continuous and 1-periodic, so the max
is attained; fix a maximizer t*.

Claim. Either ML(v) = 1/2 and t = 1/2 is a maximizer, or there exist i != j and an integer a
with t* = a/(v_i + v_j). In particular ML(v) is attained at a rational time whose reduced
denominator divides v_i + v_j <= v_{n-1} + v_n.

Sketch.
1. Each g_i is piecewise linear with slopes +-v_i, breakpoints at g_i = 0 (t in (1/v_i)Z)
   and g_i = 1/2 (t in (1/(2v_i)) Z \ (1/v_i) Z). Since ML(v) > 0 (v has gcd 1 and n >= 1,
   f is not identically 0), no active g_i (g_i(t*) = f(t*)) is at a zero-breakpoint.
2. If some active g_i has g_i(t*) = 1/2 then f(t*) = 1/2, so every runner is at distance
   exactly 1/2 at t*: v_j t* = 1/2 mod 1 for all j. Write t* = p/q reduced. Then q | 2 v_j p,
   so q | 2 v_j for all j; with gcd(v) = 1 this forces q | 2, so t* = 1/2. (q = 1 is impossible
   since then all g_j(t*) = 0.)
3. Otherwise every active g_i is linear near t* with slope s_i in {+v_i, -v_i}. Let A be the
   active set. For t near t*, f(t) = min_{i in A} (c + s_i (t - t*)) since inactive g_j are
   strictly larger near t*. Hence for t < t*: f(t) = c + (t - t*) max_A s_i, and for t > t*:
   f(t) = c + (t - t*) min_A s_i. Local maximality forces max_A s_i >= 0 and min_A s_i <= 0;
   since s_i != 0 there are i, j in A with s_i = +v_i, s_j = -v_j (i != j because a single
   g_i has one slope at a non-breakpoint). From g_i(t*) = g_j(t*): v_i t* - b_i = b_j - v_j t*
   for integers b_i, b_j, i.e. t* = (b_i + b_j)/(v_i + v_j).

Consequences (own reasoning).
(a) LRC(n) is equivalent to: for every gcd-1 n-tuple v there are i < j and a in Z with
    min_l || v_l a/(v_i+v_j) || >= 1/(n+1). Only the finitely many sum-denominators matter;
    differences v_j - v_i never need to be tested (the enumeration data agree: all 36,625
    recorded maximizing times for near-tight sets have denominators dividing some v_i+v_j).
(b) The a priori search space for a lonely time is O(n^2 * max v) rational points, versus the
    O(n^2 max v) breakpoint set including differences and 2v_i; not asymptotically smaller, but
    structurally sharper (the maximizer is a *crossing* of an ascending and a descending runner).
(c) For the loneliness spectrum: ML(v) = p/q with q | (v_i + v_j). For Kravitz-type values
    s/(ns+k) this says ns+k divides some pairwise sum of speeds.

Status. This is a short elementary argument and is very likely folklore (piecewise-linear
maxima occur at crossings; cf. the polyhedral viewpoint in Beck–Hoşten–Schymura, "Lonely Runner
Polyhedra", and Goddyn–Wong). I have not located an explicit statement in the sources I checked.
Not formalised; step 2's gcd argument and step 3's local analysis should be checked independently.
